<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Cleartext is OFF in release builds, and only in debug builds is it allowed at all.

  capacitor.config.ts sets `allowMixedContent: false`; this is the platform half of the same
  decision, and both are needed — the first stops the WEBVIEW loading http subresources, this stops
  the APP opening http connections.

  WHY `debug-overrides` IS ALL-OR-NOTHING HERE. The obvious shape — naming 10.0.2.2 and
  patient.healthconnect.local so only those two are exempt — is not expressible: Android rejects
  both `<domain-config>` and `<domain>` inside `<debug-overrides>`, which accepts nothing but
  `<trust-anchors>` and applies to every host. Per-host exemption needs a top-level
  `<domain-config>`, and that would apply to the RELEASE build too, which is strictly worse.

  So the trade is: debug builds may speak cleartext to anything; release builds may not speak it at
  all. The containment is the build type, and `debug-overrides` is stripped entirely from release —
  it cannot be reached by a misconfigured production APK.

  DO NOT set `cleartextTrafficPermitted="true"` on `base-config` to make a local gateway work. That
  is the change this comment exists to prevent: it opens cleartext everywhere, in release too, and
  nothing fails to remind you.

  Android lint catches malformed configs here, but ONLY on a release build — an earlier version of
  this file with nested `<domain-config>` survived several `assembleDebug` runs.

  The hosts this exists for: 10.0.2.2 (the emulator's alias for the host machine, for `npm start`)
  and patient.healthconnect.local (the quality stack on jacserver, http on a private LAN).
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="false">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </base-config>

    <debug-overrides cleartextTrafficPermitted="true">
        <trust-anchors>
            <certificates src="system" />
            <!--
              User-installed CAs, so a developer can put a proxy in front of the gateway to read
              traffic. Debug only — a release build trusting user CAs is how a hostile network
              reads a patient's record.
            -->
            <certificates src="user" />
        </trust-anchors>
    </debug-overrides>
</network-security-config>
